    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Mixtures: finding ratios, quantities and costs of components in a mixture.
         Suggested activity: Learners mix water and milk (or maize and beans) in different ratios and calculate the cost per kg/litre of each mixture using local market prices. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLO: Core 1.3 (d), (e), (f); Essential 1.2 (e), (f), (g). Lessons 4.1 and 4.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (mixing maize and beans, or milk and water, and costing per kg or litre at local prices)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/03-compound-proportions-rates-of-work-and-mixtures/04-mixtures/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-mixtures-in-a-ratio">
        <title>Mixtures in a Ratio</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the components of a mixture made in a ratio; the cost of 1 kg or 1 litre of a mixture.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the components; find the cost per kilogram or per litre; explain why the mean price is usually wrong.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> githeri, chai, uji flour, animal feed and fertiliser.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners mix and cost maize and beans at local prices and find when the mean of the two prices gives the right cost.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>If learners know today's prices at the local market, use them; otherwise use the prices in the activity.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-costing-githeri">
            <title>Costing Githeri</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Maize costs Ksh <m>55</m> a kilogram and beans Ksh <m>150</m> a kilogram.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Mix <m>3\,\text{kg}</m> of maize with <m>1\,\text{kg}</m> of beans. What does the mix cost, and what does 1 kg of it cost?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>3 \times 55 + 150 = 315</m>, so Ksh <m>315</m> for <m>4\,\text{kg}</m>, and <m>315 \div 4 = 78.75</m>, Ksh <m>78.75</m> a kilogram.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Mix maize and beans in the ratio <m>2 : 1</m>. What does 1 kg cost?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>3\,\text{kg}</m> cost <m>2 \times 55 + 150 = 260</m>, and <m>260 \div 3 \approx 86.67</m>, so about Ksh <m>87</m> a kilogram.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Mix them in the ratio <m>1 : 1</m>. What does 1 kg cost?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(55 + 150) \div 2 = 102.50</m>, Ksh <m>102.50</m>: the mean of the two prices.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Why is only the <m>1 : 1</m> mix costed by the mean of the prices?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>With equal masses each price counts equally. With more maize, the cheaper price counts more and pulls the cost down.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>At home, make a small mix of your own (water and milk for chai, or maize and beans) and cost 1 litre or 1 kg of it at your local prices.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Open.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the components and cost of a mixture.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> in every part, the cost of 1 kg was the total cost divided by the total mass.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The components of a mixture are found by sharing in the ratio.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The mean of the prices is right only for equal masses.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-mixtures">
            <title>Mixtures</title>
            <p>A <term>mixture</term> is made of two or more <term>components</term>. When the components are mixed in a ratio, find each component by sharing in that ratio. The cost of 1 kg (or 1 litre) of the mixture is</p>
            <p><md>\text{cost of 1 kg} = \frac{\text{total cost}}{\text{total mass}}</md></p>
            <p>It always lies between the two prices, nearer the one used more; it is the mean of the prices only when equal masses are used. The cost of a mixture leans towards the ingredient you use more of.</p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-chai-for-the-school">
            <title>Chai for the School</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The school kitchen makes <m>40\,\text{litres}</m> of milk and water for chai, in the ratio <m>7 : 3</m>. Milk costs Ksh <m>60</m> a litre (the water is not costed). How much milk is used, and what does 1 litre of the mixture cost?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>10</m> parts make <m>40\,\text{litres}</m>, so one part is <m>4\,\text{litres}</m>: milk <m>7 \times 4 = 28\,\text{litres}</m> and water <m>12\,\text{litres}</m>.</p>
                <p>The milk costs <m>28 \times 60 = 1680</m>, Ksh <m>1680</m>, so 1 litre of the mixture costs <m>1680 \div 40 = 42</m>, Ksh <m>42</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-a-coffee-blend">
            <title>A Coffee Blend</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A roaster blends <m>6\,\text{kg}</m> of grade AA coffee at Ksh <m>1600</m> a kilogram with <m>9\,\text{kg}</m> of grade AB coffee at Ksh <m>1300</m> a kilogram. What does 1 kg of the blend cost?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Total cost: <m>6 \times 1600 + 9 \times 1300 = 9600 + 11\,700 = 21\,300</m>. Total mass: <m>15\,\text{kg}</m>.</p>
                <p><m>21\,300 \div 15 = 1420</m>, so Ksh <m>1420</m> a kilogram: nearer the AB price, because more AB is used.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-dairy-meal">
            <title>Dairy Meal</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A farmer mixes maize germ and sunflower cake in the ratio <m>7 : 3</m> to make <m>300\,\text{kg}</m> of dairy meal. Maize germ costs Ksh <m>35</m> a kilogram and sunflower cake Ksh <m>60</m>. Find the mass of each and the cost of 1 kg of the meal.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>One part is <m>300 \div 10 = 30\,\text{kg}</m>: maize germ <m>7 \times 30 = 210\,\text{kg}</m> and sunflower cake <m>3 \times 30 = 90\,\text{kg}</m>.</p>
                <p>Total cost: <m>210 \times 35 + 90 \times 60 = 7350 + 5400 = 12\,750</m>, so 1 kg costs <m>12\,750 \div 300 = 42.50</m>, Ksh <m>42.50</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example <c>45326</c>. Give a fraction as a fraction, for example <c>7/12</c>, or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-githeri-components-and-cost">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-githeri-components-and-cost" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/03-compound-proportions-rates-of-work-and-mixtures/04-mixtures/Githeri-components-and-cost.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-mean-price-claim">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-mean-price-claim" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/03-compound-proportions-rates-of-work-and-mixtures/04-mixtures/Mean-price-claim.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-mixtures-in-a-ratio">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-feed-three-parts">
                <statement><p>A <m>90\,\text{kg}</m> bag of feed contains maize bran, cotton seed cake and minerals in the ratio <m>12 : 5 : 1</m>. Find the mass of each.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>60\,\text{kg}</m>, <m>25\,\text{kg}</m> and <m>5\,\text{kg}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-rice-two-prices">
                <statement><p><m>20\,\text{kg}</m> of rice at Ksh <m>140</m> a kilogram is mixed with <m>30\,\text{kg}</m> at Ksh <m>190</m>. Find the cost of 1 kg of the mixture.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Ksh <m>170</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>(20 \times 140 + 30 \times 190) \div 50 = 8500 \div 50 = 170</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-uji-flour-three">
                <statement><p>Uji flour is made of maize, millet and sorghum flour in the ratio <m>5 : 4 : 1</m>, <m>30\,\text{kg}</m> in all. They cost Ksh <m>70</m>, Ksh <m>200</m> and Ksh <m>140</m> a kilogram. Find the mass of each and the cost of 1 kg of the uji flour.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>15\,\text{kg}</m>, <m>12\,\text{kg}</m> and <m>3\,\text{kg}</m>; Ksh <m>129</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Total cost <m>15 \times 70 + 12 \times 200 + 3 \times 140 = 1050 + 2400 + 420 = 3870</m>, and <m>3870 \div 30 = 129</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-chai-36-litres">
                <statement><p>A kitchen makes <m>36\,\text{litres}</m> of milk and water in the ratio <m>2 : 1</m>. Milk costs Ksh <m>65</m> a litre. Find the milk used and the cost of 1 litre of the mixture.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>24\,\text{litres}</m>; Ksh <m>43</m> to the nearest shilling</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Milk <m>24\,\text{litres}</m> costs <m>24 \times 65 = 1560</m>, and <m>1560 \div 36 \approx 43.33</m>, so Ksh <m>43</m> a litre to the nearest shilling.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-fertiliser-blend">
                <statement><p>A farmer blends DAP and CAN fertiliser in the ratio <m>7 : 5</m> to make <m>420\,\text{kg}</m>. They cost Ksh <m>72</m> and Ksh <m>54</m> a kilogram. Find the mass of each and the cost of 1 kg of the blend.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>245\,\text{kg}</m> and <m>175\,\text{kg}</m>; Ksh <m>64.50</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Total cost <m>245 \times 72 + 175 \times 54 = 17\,640 + 9450 = 27\,090</m>, and <m>27\,090 \div 420 = 64.50</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-equal-money-not-equal-mass">
                <title>Challenge: Equal Money, Not Equal Mass</title>
                <statement><p>Show that mixing equal masses of two foods gives the mean of their prices, but spending equal amounts of money on each does not.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Try Ksh <m>600</m> on each of two foods at Ksh <m>60</m> and Ksh <m>150</m> a kilogram.</p></hint>
                <answer><p>Equal masses <m>m</m>: <m>\dfrac{mp + mq}{2m} = \dfrac{p + q}{2}</m>. Ksh <m>600</m> on each buys <m>10\,\text{kg}</m> and <m>4\,\text{kg}</m>: Ksh <m>1200</m> for <m>14\,\text{kg}</m>, and <m>1200 \div 14 \approx 85.71</m>, so about Ksh <m>86</m> a kilogram, not Ksh <m>105</m>.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
