    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-mixing-to-a-target-price">
        <title>Mixing to a Target Price</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the balance rule for a target price; profit as a percentage of the cost price.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the ratio in which to mix two grades for a target price; find how much of one grade to add; work back from a selling price and a percentage profit.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> traders' blends of coffee, tea, rice and beans.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Curriculum Links</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>Revisit the key inquiry questions (<xref ref="kiq-proportions"/>) with <xref ref="insight-proportion-in-daily-life"/>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Critical thinking and problem solving (checking that each answer makes sense); communication and collaboration (group work in every activity).</p></li>
                <li><title>Values</title><p component="core">Responsibility; respect.</p><p component="essential">Social justice; respect.</p></li>
                <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Citizenship: equity and non-discrimination, in fair shares by contribution and by work done, safe working hours, and honest mixtures sold at stated prices.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find by trial the mixes of two coffee grades that cost Ksh <m>1400</m> and Ksh <m>1500</m> a kilogram, and discover the balance rule.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Ask: <q>How far is each grade's price from the target?</q></p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-hitting-the-target-price">
            <title>Hitting the Target Price</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A trader sells grade AA coffee at Ksh <m>1600</m> a kilogram and grade AB at Ksh <m>1300</m> a kilogram.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the cost of 1 kg of AA and AB mixed in the ratios <m>1 : 1</m>, <m>2 : 1</m> and <m>1 : 2</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Ksh <m>1450</m>, Ksh <m>1500</m> and Ksh <m>1400</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Which mix costs Ksh <m>1400</m> a kilogram, and which Ksh <m>1500</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>AA : AB <m>= 1 : 2</m> costs Ksh <m>1400</m>; AA : AB <m>= 2 : 1</m> costs Ksh <m>1500</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>For Ksh <m>1400</m>: each kilogram of AA is Ksh <m>200</m> above the target and each kilogram of AB is Ksh <m>100</m> below it. How many kilograms of AB balance 1 kg of AA?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>2\,\text{kg}</m>, since <m>2 \times 100 = 200</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the balance rule, the equation method and profit on cost.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) is the balance rule: distances from the target, crossed over.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Always check the ratio by costing the mixture.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Profit is a percentage of the cost price, not of the selling price.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-mixing-to-a-target-price">
            <title>Mixing to a Target Price</title>
            <p>To mix a cheap grade and a dear grade so that the mixture costs a target price, balance the distances from the target:</p>
            <p><md>(\text{target} - \text{cheap price}) \times \text{mass of cheap} = (\text{dear price} - \text{target}) \times \text{mass of dear}</md></p>
            <p>so cheap : dear <m>=</m> (dear price <m>-</m> target) : (target <m>-</m> cheap price). The same rule comes from an equation: the total cost of the mixture equals the target price times its total mass.</p>
            <p>When a mixture is sold at a profit of <m>r\%</m> on the cost, selling price <m>=</m> cost price <m>\times \left(1 + \dfrac{r}{100}\right)</m>, so cost price <m>=</m> selling price <m>\div \left(1 + \dfrac{r}{100}\right)</m>.</p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-price-line">
            <caption>Balancing two grades of coffee on a price line.</caption>
            <image width="70%">
                <shortdescription>On a price line the target 1400 is 100 above AB and 200 below AA, so 2 kg of AB balance 1 kg of AA.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
% SET: 1 unit = 50 shillings; the line runs from 1300 (x = 0) to 1600 (x = 6).
% CALCULATE: target 1400 is at x = 2; distances 100 and 200.
% DRAW: the price line with ticks every 50; points at AB, the target and AA.
% MARK: measuring lines for the two distances. LABEL: grades, prices and the balance.
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
\draw[thick] (-0.3,0) -- (6.3,0);
\foreach \i/\p in {0/1300,1/1350,2/1400,3/1450,4/1500,5/1550,6/1600} { \draw (\i,-0.1) -- (\i,0.1); \node[below, font=\small] at (\i,-0.15) {$\p$}; }
\fill (0,0) circle (3pt); \fill (6,0) circle (3pt);
\draw[very thick] (2,-0.25) -- (2,0.25);
\node[above] at (0,0.3) {AB};
\node[above] at (6,0.3) {AA};
\node[above] at (2,0.3) {target $1400$};
\draw[|-|, thick] (0,1.1) -- (2,1.1); \node[above] at (1,1.15) {$100$ below};
\draw[|-|, thick] (2,1.1) -- (6,1.1); \node[above] at (4,1.15) {$200$ above};
\node[right, font=\small] at (6.4,0) {Ksh a kg};
\node[below] at (3,-0.8) {balance: $2$ kg of AB $\times\ 100 = 1$ kg of AA $\times\ 200$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-tea-grades">
            <title>Tea Grades</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A tea packer mixes grade PF1 tea at Ksh <m>520</m> a kilogram with grade BP1 at Ksh <m>680</m> a kilogram, so that the blend costs Ksh <m>590</m> a kilogram. In what ratio are they mixed?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>PF1 is <m>590 - 520 = 70</m> below the target and BP1 is <m>680 - 590 = 90</m> above it. Crossing over:</p>
                <p>PF1 : BP1 <m>= 90 : 70 = 9 : 7</m>.</p>
                <p>Check: <m>(9 \times 520 + 7 \times 680) \div 16 = (4680 + 4760) \div 16 = 590</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-how-much-to-add">
            <title>How Much to Add?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A trader has <m>40\,\text{kg}</m> of beans that cost Ksh <m>120</m> a kilogram. How many kilograms of beans at Ksh <m>160</m> must be added so that the mixture costs Ksh <m>150</m> a kilogram?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Let <m>x</m> kg be added. The total cost equals <m>150</m> times the total mass:</p>
                <p><md>4800 + 160x = 150(40 + x)</md> so <m>4800 + 160x = 6000 + 150x</m>, <m>10x = 1200</m> and <m>x = 120\,\text{kg}</m>.</p>
                <p>By the balance rule: <m>40 \times 30 = x \times 10</m>, which gives the same answer.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-mixing-for-a-profit">
            <title>Mixing for a Profit</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A shopkeeper mixes rice that cost Ksh <m>150</m> and Ksh <m>210</m> a kilogram and sells the mixture at Ksh <m>222</m> a kilogram, making a profit of <m>20\%</m> on the cost. In what ratio is the rice mixed?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The cost price of the mixture is <m>222 \div 1.2 = 185</m>, Ksh <m>185</m> a kilogram.</p>
                <p>Cheap : dear <m>= (210 - 185) : (185 - 150) = 25 : 35 = 5 : 7</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <insight xml:id="insight-proportion-in-daily-life">
            <title>Proportion in Daily Life</title>
            <p>Ratios and proportions let a group share a profit fairly by what each member put in; they let a contractor plan workers and days, and a company plan drivers so that no one drives for too long; and they let a trader price a mixture honestly, so that customers pay for what they get. How fast people and machines work decides how many are needed, and how long they can safely work.</p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type numbers only, without Ksh, units, spaces or commas, for example <c>45326</c>. Give a fraction as a fraction, for example <c>7/12</c>, or as a decimal correct to 3 decimal places. Give times, masses and volumes exactly or correct to 1 decimal place, and money to the nearest shilling, unless the question says otherwise.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-mixing-ratio-for-a-target-price">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-mixing-ratio-for-a-target-price" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/03-compound-proportions-rates-of-work-and-mixtures/04-mixtures/Mixing-ratio-for-a-target-price.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-adding-a-dearer-grade-for-a-profit">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-adding-a-dearer-grade-for-a-profit" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/03-compound-proportions-rates-of-work-and-mixtures/04-mixtures/Adding-a-dearer-grade-for-a-profit.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-mixing-to-a-target-price">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-coffee-1200-1700">
                <statement><p>In what ratio must coffee at Ksh <m>1200</m> and Ksh <m>1700</m> a kilogram be mixed so that the mixture costs Ksh <m>1500</m> a kilogram?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>2 : 3</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>(1700 - 1500) : (1500 - 1200) = 200 : 300 = 2 : 3</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-maize-48-60">
                <statement><p>Maize at Ksh <m>48</m> and Ksh <m>60</m> a kilogram is mixed so that the mixture costs Ksh <m>52</m> a kilogram. In what ratio?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>2 : 1</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-milk-add-dearer">
                <statement><p><m>30\,\text{litres}</m> of milk at Ksh <m>55</m> a litre are mixed with milk at Ksh <m>70</m> a litre so that the mixture costs Ksh <m>60</m> a litre. How many litres at Ksh <m>70</m> are used?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>15\,\text{litres}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>30 \times 5 = x \times 10</m>, so <m>x = 15</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-tea-for-profit">
                <statement><p>Tea at Ksh <m>500</m> and Ksh <m>800</m> a kilogram is mixed and sold at Ksh <m>702</m> a kilogram, a profit of <m>17\%</m> on the cost. Find the cost of 1 kg of the mixture and the ratio.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Ksh <m>600</m>; <m>2 : 1</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>702 \div 1.17 = 600</m>, and <m>(800 - 600) : (600 - 500) = 200 : 100 = 2 : 1</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-chai-change-ratio">
                <statement><p><m>28\,\text{litres}</m> of milk and water for chai are in the ratio <m>5 : 2</m>. How much milk must be added to make the ratio <m>7 : 2</m>?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>8\,\text{litres}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Milk <m>20\,\text{litres}</m> and water <m>8\,\text{litres}</m>. With <m>8\,\text{litres}</m> of water, <m>7 : 2</m> needs <m>28\,\text{litres}</m> of milk, so add <m>8\,\text{litres}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-impossible-target">
                <title>Challenge: An Impossible Target</title>
                <statement><p>Why can no mixture of maize at Ksh <m>48</m> and Ksh <m>60</m> a kilogram cost Ksh <m>65</m> a kilogram?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>The cost of a mixture always lies between the two prices, here between Ksh <m>48</m> and Ksh <m>60</m>.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-mixing-to-a-target-price">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>The activity, <xref ref="fig-price-line"/> and <xref ref="example-tea-grades"/>. Give a card: <q>cross the distances</q>. Assign <xref ref="ex-coffee-1200-1700"/> and <xref ref="ex-maize-48-60"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-coffee-1200-1700"/> to <xref ref="ex-chai-change-ratio"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-impossible-target"/>. Extension (digital): build a spreadsheet that costs any mix of two grades and finds the ratio for a target price typed in.</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>


